keys, AWS describes them as IAM-backed service-specific credentials and does not state the same region restriction in the same way. citeturn859146view0turn859146view1\n\nAlso, AWS’s generic “supported regions for Bedrock API keys” page currently lists `us-east-1` but **not `us-east-2`**, while AWS separately documents the **Mantle endpoint itself as available in Ohio**. That mismatch is exactly the sort of AWS documentation/product-surface inconsistency we need to test empirically rather than reason around. citeturn699882search0turn699882search7\n\n## Don: do **not** create another key yet\n\nWe already have enough credentials. Creating a fourth key adds another variable.\n\nThe next clean move is to use **one existing active key**, preferably the newest:\n\n`BedrockAPIKey-ios2-at-28...`\n\nIf you still have its actual secret stored locally, use that one.\n\nIf you **do not** have the secret value anymore, AWS will not normally reveal the full value again. In that case, then yes, we generate **one fresh test key** and document exactly where/when we created it.\n\n## First PowerShell diagnostic block\n\nRun this exactly. It exposes no secret values:\n\n```powershell\nWrite-Host \"=== AWS IDENTITY ===\"\naws sts get-caller-identity\n\nWrite-Host \"`n=== AWS CLI REGION ===\"\naws configure get region\n\nWrite-Host \"`n=== ENVIRONMENT REGIONS ===\"\nWrite-Host \"AWS_REGION=$env:AWS_REGION\"\nWrite-Host \"AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=$env:AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\"\n\nWrite-Host \"`n=== BEDROCK / OPENAI ENV STATUS ===\"\n\nif ($env:AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK) {\n    Write-Host \"AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=PRESENT length=$($env:AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK.Length)\"\n} else {\n    Write-Host \"AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=ABSENT\"\n}\n\nif ($env:OPENAI_API_KEY) {\n    Write-Host \"OPENAI_API_KEY=PRESENT length=$($env:OPENAI_API_KEY.Length)\"\n} else {\n    Write-Host \"OPENAI_API_KEY=ABSENT\"\n}\n\nWrite-Host \"OPENAI_BASE_URL=$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL\"\n```\n\nPaste me the output.\n\n---\n\n## Then we do one controlled test\n\nOnce we know which secret you actually have locally, the clean Mantle test will look like this:\n\n```powershell\n$BaseUrl = \"https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\"\n$Model   = \"moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\"\n```\n\nThen a microscopic POST to:\n\n```text\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1/chat/completions\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"model\": \"moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": \"Return exactly: BEDROCK_OK\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_tokens\": 20\n}\n```\n\nBut **don’t run that yet** until we establish whether your local secret is in:\n\n```text\nAWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK\n```\n\nor:\n\n```text\nOPENAI_API_KEY\n```\n\nbecause I don’t want us silently testing the wrong credential again.\n\n## Current working diagnosis\n\nWe now have four separate facts:\n\n**Billing:** good — $159.26 remains.\n\n**Account:** good — correct account.\n\n**Mantle console:** good — Ohio endpoint surface exists.\n\n**Credentials:** at least three long-term keys exist and show Active.\n\nSo the remaining failure surface is getting much smaller:\n\n> **secret actually being used → region → endpoint → model ID → IAM permission**\n\nThat is absolutely solvable.\n\nRun the diagnostic block above and paste the output. After that I’ll give you the exact `Invoke-RestMethod` call for **Test A: Ohio + Mantle + Kimi K2.5**, with no SDK or OpenCode involved.", "text_chars": 4134, "code_blocks": 6, "links": ["https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1", "https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1/chat/completions"], "citations": ["https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1", "https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1/chat/completions"], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e7760d55-185c-42d1-a7da-25219dbfe172", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215544.688392, "updated_at