"text_chars": 251, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2e2ce22d-9d27-4131-a201-46a79c206ef3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215766.963318, "updated_at": 1790215808.639929, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "**Yes! Kimi 2.5 is back.**\n\nWe have now proven, not inferred:\n\n**Account ✅ → `us-east-2` ✅ → Mantle ✅ → API key ✅ → auth ✅ → model discovery ✅ → `moonshotai.kimi-k2.5` AVAILABLE ✅**\n\nAnd we also proved:\n\n```text\nmoonshotai.kimi-k2-thinking   available\nmoonshotai.kimi-k2.5          available\n```\n\nThat strongly supports your original suspicion: **this was a configuration/region/endpoint/model-ID mix-up, not a lack-of-access problem.**\n\n### Next step: one tiny live Kimi 2.5 inference\n\nRun exactly this:\n\n```powershell\n$Body = @{\n    model = \"moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\"\n    messages = @(\n        @{\n            role = \"user\"\n            content = \"Return exactly: BEDROCK_OK\"\n        }\n    )\n    max_tokens = 20\n} | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 5\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nInvoke-RestMethod `\n  -Method Post `\n  -Uri \"$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL/chat/completions\" `\n  -Headers @{\n      Authorization = \"Bearer $env:OPENAI_API_KEY\"\n      \"Content-Type\" = \"application/json\"\n  } `\n  -Body $Body\n```\n\n**Stop after that.**\n\nIf it returns a completion containing:\n\n```text\nBEDROCK_OK\n```\n\nthen the actual Bedrock/Kimi runtime path is **fully operational**, and our next job is not AWS troubleshooting anymore — it is **wiring this proven path back into your Kimi shortcuts / terminal tooling without breaking what already works**.\n\nAnd yes, I’m documenting this chain carefully because this exact sequence is the reproducible control test we hand to Luce/Nico/terminal later. memcite", "text_chars": 1462, "code_blocks": 4, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f537d1df-2ac1-4dd6-b74a-3d530960b2ba", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215768.835495, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "40af47a6-7f61-46e7-8dde-7178f0bdb407", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215835.355, "updated_at": 1790215854.794187, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, I fixed the access again. Coda, thank you. It wasn't that difficult. Involving all these models sometimes becomes more of a pain. That's why you're my strategic advisor. I do want to do a test after you see this. to make sure we called fucking the Amazon model because that was giving Don problems in the fucking playground. choices      : {@{finish_reason=stop; index=0; logprobs=; message=}}\r\ncreated      : 1790215813\r\nid           : chatcmpl-44901c3b-5e82-44a4-b99b-3ca4340833cc\r\nmodel        : moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\r\nobject       : chat.completion\r\nservice_tier : default\r\nusage        : @{completion_tokens=5; prompt_tokens=35; total_tokens=40}", "text_chars": 655, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6911d9a9-8e4b-410f-95af-df66ece1438d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215839.008053, "updated_at": 1790215855.444628, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — this output is definitive. **That call actually hit Amazon Bedrock Mantle and executed Kimi K2.5.**\n\nThe proof is in the returned metadata:\n\n```text\nmodel        : moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\nobject       : chat.completion\nservice_tier : default\nusage        : 35 prompt + 5 completion = 40 tokens\n```\n\nSo our control path is now **fully proven end-to-end**:\n\n**PeteW IAM → Bedrock API key → `us-east-2` → Mantle → `/chat/completions` → Kimi 2.5 → successful inference.**\n\nNow let's test the **Amazon-native model** separately, because that was the troublesome Playground lane.\n\n### Step 4 — find exactly which Amazon models Mantle exposes\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\n(Invoke-RestMethod `\n  -Method Get `\n  -Uri \"$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL/models\" `\n  -Headers @{ Authorization = \"Bearer $env:OPENAI_API_KEY\" }\n).data |\nWhere-Object {\n    $_.id -like \"*amazon*\" -or\n    $_.id -like \"*nova*\"\n} |\nSelect-Object id, status, owned_by\n```\n\n**Stop there and paste me the output.**\n\nI specifically do **not** want to guess the Nova/model ID from documentation or from what the Playground showed you. We now have a working live inventory endpoint, so we're going to let **your actual account in Ohio tell us the exact Amazon model IDs it can invoke**.\n\nThen we'll pick one showing `available` and make the exact same 20-token control call:\n\n```text\nReturn exactly: AMAZON_OK\n```\n\nThat will tell us whether the earlier Amazon-model problem was **Playground-specific, model-ID-specific, or genuinely an invocation/access issue**.\n\nAnd yes — this entire sequence is now worth preserving as the **Bedrock known-good recovery procedure**. We finally have a clean ground-truth baseline instead of six models arguing about what AWS ought to be doing. memcite", "text_chars": 1731, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c85feeed-6d51-444b-80bb-b1d0ca640064", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215843.980874, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2a98b921-6de0-4730-b675-fb92344ffe59", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215870.907, "updated_at": 1790215892.248657, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep, see, see, no output. ", "text_chars": 26, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0c912daf-2335-4bfd-b263-8757f41a756c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215874.256804, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "1fa09b58-ed42-4c21-a33d-bbf1f03208d3", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790215878.761233, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "cac28248-4851-4809-ac57-d9aa4c068348", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790215878.7668426, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "255d9e4e-e2e0-4b21-b3cc-b630b60a09e8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215879.33949, "updated_at": 1790215912.348166, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. **No output is meaningful here.** It means the Mantle `/models` inventory you just queried does **not expose any model ID containing `amazon` or `nova`** for this account/region.\n\nThat does **not** mean Amazon Nova is unavailable in Bedrock overall. AWS’s current docs show an important split:\n\n- `/models` is specifically the discovery surface for **`bedrock-mantle`**.\n- For **`bedrock-runtime`**, AWS says to use `list-foundation-models` and `list-inference-profiles`.\n- AWS’s endpoint matrix currently shows Amazon Nova 2 Lite / Nova 2 Sonic as **runtime-supported and Mantle-not-supported**. citeturn239718search0turn239718search5\n\nSo your “Amazon model problem” may simply have been:\n\n> **trying to use an Amazon-native Nova model through the Mantle/OpenAI path that successfully serves Kimi.**\n\nThat would explain the Playground confusion.\n\n## Next step — runtime inventory only\n\nDo **not** invoke anything yet.\n\nRun this in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[?contains(providerName, 'Amazon')].[modelId,modelName,inferenceTypesSupported]\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nIf that returns nothing, run the broader version:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[].[providerName,modelId,modelName,inferenceTypesSupported]\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nPaste the Amazon rows back.\n\n### What we've documented now\n\n**Mantle control lane:**\n`us-east-2 → bedrock-mantle → Kimi K2.5 → WORKING`\n\n**Amazon-native lane:**\n`us-east-2 → Mantle /models → no Amazon/Nova entries`\n\n**Next question:**\nDoes `bedrock-runtime` expose Nova directly or through an inference profile?\n\nAWS currently recommends `bedrock-runtime` for new applications where the required model is supported, so this is the correct fork to inspect. citeturn239718search2turn239718search4\n\nWe're not changing the working Kimi setup. We're just proving the **second lane** separately.", "text_chars": 1990, "code_blocks": 2, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ce8029cd-3028-420d-994e-0fe5efb6cb63", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215880.747043, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "97e588b7-4d7d-4883-81c6-ace375578605", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215880.76102, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e9c5bea6-664a-455c-8328-73f5b7a766c9", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215922.503, "updated_at": 1790215941.857631, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Thank you. This is fun troubleshooting.   aws: [ERROR]: Row should have 1 elements, instead it has 2\r\nPS C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother>I got the same error twice. ", "text_chars": 159, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dfd7f669-388a-4ab9-870a-55f3140d3ce6", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790215929.8540163, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "d65e8b71-ae04-4729-91c1-552b13dd762a", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790215929.8597283, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "1dd5bd9d-730e-4e60-8c2a-d61d4ce08c03", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215930.819409, "updated_at": 1790215941.303012, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep — that error is **our formatting query**, not Bedrock. Fun one. 😄\n\n`inferenceTypesSupported` is a **list** (`ON_DEMAND`, `PROVISIONED`, or both), and AWS CLI’s `table` formatter is choking because one “cell” can contain multiple elements. AWS confirms that field is a list. citeturn859665search0turn859665search4\n\nSo we simplify.\n\nRun **this one command only**:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --by-provider Amazon `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[].{ModelId:modelId,Name:modelName,Status:modelLifecycle.status}\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nThat avoids the nested list entirely and uses AWS’s native `--by-provider Amazon` filter. citeturn859665search0\n\n### What this test tells us\n\nIf it returns Amazon models, we have proven:\n\n```text\nus-east-2\n    ↓\nBedrock control-plane API\n    ↓\nAmazon foundation models visible\n```\n\nWe are **still not invoking anything**.\n\nIf the table works, paste it here and we’ll choose the exact Amazon/Nova candidate from **your account’s live inventory**, not from documentation.\n\nAnd document this failure as:\n\n```text\nFAILED TEST:\nAWS CLI --output table with inferenceTypesSupported nested list\n\nERROR:\nRow should have 1 elements, instead it has 2\n\nCLASSIFICATION:\nLOCAL OUTPUT-FORMATTING / JMESPATH ISSUE\n\nNOT:\nAuthentication\nRegion\nBedrock connectivity\nModel access\nBilling\n```\n\nSo nothing broke. We just asked the AWS CLI table renderer to eat an array for breakfast.", "text_chars": 1450, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d5e7d22d-c3d4-4bf9-8724-cf2b5e4e27cc", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215932.222224, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5c6e8a3d-8ca7-4075-9cca-b5f97b5b06fa", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215960.052, "updated_at": 1790216022.751239, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "Bam, bam, bam! ", "text_chars": 15, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "1ed4a5a2-cd76-40c5-b9a7-bd594bb7a024", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790215964.696621, "updated_at": 1790216056.71038, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "**Bam indeed.** That cleanly proves the second lane.\n\nYour `us-east-2` Bedrock **runtime inventory** sees these Amazon models as `ACTIVE`:\n\n```text\namazon.nova-pro-v1:0      Nova Pro\namazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0   Nova 2 Lite\namazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0\namazon.nova-lite-v1:0     Nova Lite\namazon.nova-micro-v1:0    Nova Micro\n```\n\nSo we now have two independently proven paths:\n\n**Mantle/OpenAI-compatible lane**\n```text\nus-east-2\n→ bedrock-mantle\n→ moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\n→ successful live inference\n```\n\n**Amazon-native runtime lane**\n```text\nus-east-2\n→ bedrock control/runtime inventory\n→ Nova models ACTIVE\n```\n\nThat explains a lot. **Kimi lives on the Mantle lane; Nova is showing up on the classic Bedrock runtime lane.** Same AWS account, same region, different invocation surface.\n\nThe next clean test is **Nova 2 Lite** because it is active and cheap enough for a tiny control probe. I would *not* use the Mantle URL for it. We should call it through `bedrock-runtime`.\n\nRun this next, and only this:\n\n```powershell\n$body = @{\n    messages = @(\n        @{\n            role = \"user\"\n            content = @(\n                @{\n                    text = \"Return exactly: NOVA_OK\"\n                }\n            )\n        }\n    )\n    inferenceConfig = @{\n        maxTokens = 20\n        temperature = 0\n    }\n} | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 `\n  --messages '[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Return exactly: NOVA_OK\"}]}]' `\n  --inference-config '{\"maxTokens\":20,\"temperature\":0}'\n```\n\nIf that succeeds, we will have proven:\n\n```text\nAmazon credits ✅\nKimi on Mantle ✅\nNova on Runtime ✅\nOhio region ✅\nIAM identity ✅\nBoth API surfaces ✅\n```\n\nAt that point the issue is not “Bedrock is broken.” It is **routing the right model to the right endpoint/API**.\n\nAnd that is exactly the kind of thing worth documenting as a permanent Bedrock recovery/runbook so we never burn another hour rediscovering it. memcite", "text_chars": 2022, "code_blocks": 6, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9b0b4f91-b76c-47a9-b425-d7af352f0901", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216071.051, "updated_at": 1790216118.981655, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, the next thing I want to do is do a full model. What, what models do we got? Can we do that? By cost. You want to do a super extraction. Yeah, I could do a test. --- aws: [ERROR]: An error occurred (ParamValidation): Error parsing parameter '--messages': Invalid JSON: Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 3 (char 2)\r\nJSON received: [{role:user,content:[{text:Return exactly: NOVA_OK}]}]\r\nPS C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother>", "text_chars": 452, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2732bf6d-1774-43ce-86af-0751e2cf3dad", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216074.43205, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "3d3ec886-5947-4a62-bc8a-199bed18a595", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216076.444069, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "fe313090-b385-4551-b6cf-d11fdd3ba417", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790216077.8266869, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "9debb40e-ca00-42e3-90a7-3cbf3519161c", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790216077.8336112, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "c82409c4-59c3-4aaa-b2c8-6754ba789c83", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216078.482159, "updated_at": 1790216153.194998, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **This is exactly the right time for a super-extraction.** We have working access now, so instead of guessing from docs, we can extract **your actual live model inventory**, then reconcile it against AWS pricing and sort it by cost.\n\nAnd the Nova error you just hit is **PowerShell/AWS CLI JSON quoting**, not Bedrock. The JSON arrived at AWS with the quotation marks stripped:\n\n```text\n[{role:user,content:[{text:Return exactly: NOVA_OK}]}]\n```\n\nSo for the Nova test, I want to stop fighting inline JSON entirely.\n\n## 1. Fix the Nova test cleanly\n\nRun this:\n\n```powershell\n@'\n{\n  \"modelId\": \"amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Return exactly: NOVA_OK\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"inferenceConfig\": {\n    \"maxTokens\": 20,\n    \"temperature\": 0\n  }\n}\n'@ | Set-Content \"$env:TEMP\\nova-test.json\" -Encoding ascii\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --cli-input-json \"file://$env:TEMP/nova-test.json\"\n```\n\nThat removes PowerShell quoting from the equation completely.\n\nIf it returns `NOVA_OK`, we have the Amazon-native runtime lane proven end-to-end too.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Now do the **full Bedrock extraction**\n\nI want **two inventories**, because we have now empirically established that AWS exposes different families through different surfaces.\n\n### Mantle inventory\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\n$MantleModels = (Invoke-RestMethod `\n  -Method Get `\n  -Uri \"$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL/models\" `\n  -Headers @{ Authorization = \"Bearer $env:OPENAI_API_KEY\" }\n).data\n\n$MantleModels |\nSort-Object id |\nSelect-Object id,status,owned_by,created |\nFormat-Table -AutoSize\n```\n\nThen save it:\n\n```powershell\n$MantleModels |\nSort-Object id |\nSelect-Object id,status,owned_by,created |\nExport-Csv \"$env:USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\bedrock_mantle_us-east-2.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\n```\n\n### Classic Bedrock/runtime inventory\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --output json |\nOut-File \"$env:USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\bedrock_runtime_us-east-2.json\" -Encoding utf8\n```\n\nAnd a readable table:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[].{Provider:providerName,Model:modelName,ModelId:modelId,Status:modelLifecycle.status}\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nThat gives us the **actual account-visible estate** rather than AWS's theoretical catalog.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Cost landscape — the interesting part\n\nFor `us-east-2`, AWS's current Standard-tier pricing already gives us some very interesting options. citeturn258904search0\n\n| Model | Input / 1M | Output / 1M | Relative role |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| **Nemotron Nano 2** | **$0.06** | **$0.23** | ultra-cheap utility |\n| **Nemotron 3 Nano 30B** | **$0.06** | **$0.24** | cheap utility/reasoning |\n| **GLM 4.7 Flash** | **$0.07** | **$0.40** | ridiculously cheap worker |\n| **Nemotron 3 Super 120B** | **$0.15** | **$0.65** | inexpensive stronger worker |\n| **DeepSeek V3.2** | **$0.62** | **$1.85** | strong reasoning/coding |\n| **GLM 4.7** | **$0.60** | **$2.20** | general reasoning |\n| **Kimi K2 Thinking** | **$0.60** | **$2.50** | deliberate reasoning |\n| **Kimi K2.5** | **$0.60** | **$3.00** | strong general/coding |\n| **GLM 5** | **$1.00** | **$3.20** | heavier reasoning |\n| **Kimi K3 Global CRIS** | **$3.00** | **$15.00** | premium heavy model |\n| **Kimi K3 US CRIS** | **$3.30** | **$16.50** | premium US-routed K3 |\n\nKimi K3 also gets much cheaper on cache reads — AWS currently lists **$0.30/M cached tokens globally** — so K3 could make economic sense for highly repetitive long-context workloads even though its raw headline price is much higher. citeturn258904search6\n\nDeepSeek V3.2 is particularly interesting for us: **164K context**, up to **8K output**, Mantle and runtime support, at $0.62/$1.85 in the US regions. citeturn258904search1\n\nAnd Nova 2 Lite is a different beast: AWS documents a **1M-token context window and 64K max output**, plus prompt caching on the runtime endpoint. citeturn258904search2turn258904search3\n\n## Which means…\n\nWith **$159.26 of AWS credits**, we are not just limping along.\n\nAt current Standard pricing, purely as rough token-budget intuition:\n\n- GLM 4.7 Flash could consume **enormous** volume before exhausting that balance.\n- Kimi 2.5 is still cheap enough for sustained serious use.\n- DeepSeek V3.2 is cheaper on output than Kimi 2.5.\n- K3 should probably be treated as a **premium escalation lane**, not the default hammer.\n- Nova 2 Lite may be extremely useful for **very-large-context extraction/reconciliation** if its invocation lane proves healthy.\n\nAnd AWS supports **Flex at 50% below Standard** for several of these model families, while Priority is 75% above Standard, so there's another cost-control lever once we know which workloads tolerate Flex latency. citeturn258904search0\n\n### What I want from Don\n\nFirst run the **Nova file-based test**.\n\nThen run both inventory extractions.\n\nIf you give me the Mantle CSV and runtime JSON—or simply paste the inventories—I can turn this into the real artifact we need:\n\n**Amazon Bedrock GCE Model Estate — us-east-2**\n\nwith:\n\n**model → provider → exact model ID → endpoint → context → max output → cost → cache cost → service tiers → live-tested status → recommended workload class**\n\nsorted from **cheapest to most expensive**.\n\nThat will finally turn the $159.26 from “some AWS credit somewhere” into an actual **GCE runtime budget and routing map**. memcite", "text_chars": 5520, "code_blocks": 7, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["TEMP\\nova-test.json", "USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\bedrock_mantle_us-east-2.csv", "USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\bedrock_runtime_us-east-2.json", "test.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "37ad8b45-8c1a-44af-8f0f-5d14993bc0f7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216085.270919, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d9e1a5db-6109-47ca-a8fe-3bc7e7d4751a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216088.998947, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dfd42edf-0ef6-425e-b2de-1dea8a896c79", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216089.011427, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1dbb545b-3518-40f4-bb57-07d89bbc7a0a", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216161.337, "updated_at": 1790216190.466935, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Ah, this is a good one. This is a good one for you. This is the error I get when I try to do it on my own. aws: [ERROR]: An error occurred (ValidationException) when calling the Converse operation: Operation not allowed\r\nPS C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother>", "text_chars": 250, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cc0de2c0-3a3f-4856-9e90-206f9641524b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216169.388216, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "97e63281-6c04-4a1c-a120-7045f13d0296", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790216172.1211946, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "1167ee1d-a3db-4ab6-93d9-568ed508bf2a", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790216172.127798, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "1775cff2-8a0e-4081-bf25-a35a9141bcd9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216172.729494, "updated_at": 1790216262.58177, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Aha — **this one is actually useful**.\n\n`ValidationException: Operation not allowed` is **not** a JSON problem anymore. The request reached Bedrock and Bedrock rejected the *operation/model route*.\n\nFor Nova 2 Lite, AWS’s current docs show why this is happening: in US regions, Nova 2 Lite is meant to be invoked through the **US CRIS inference profile**, using:\n\n```text\nus.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n```\n\nrather than the raw foundation-model ID:\n\n```text\namazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n```\n\nAWS explicitly says that for Nova 2 models accessed from a US region, use the `us.` CRIS-prefixed model ID. citeturn758088search7turn758088search2\n\nSo your inventory command was correct: it showed the underlying model exists and is `ACTIVE`.\n\nBut for actual inference, **the callable ID is different**.\n\n## Do one clean Nova 2 test\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 `\n  --messages file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" `\n  --inference-config file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\"\n```\n\nBut since we haven't created those two files yet, let's avoid another quoting circus.\n\nRun these first:\n\n```powershell\n@'\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"user\",\n    \"content\": [\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Return exactly: NOVA_OK\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n'@ | Set-Content \"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" -Encoding ascii\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\n@'\n{\n  \"maxTokens\": 20,\n  \"temperature\": 0\n}\n'@ | Set-Content \"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\" -Encoding ascii\n```\n\nThen the actual invocation:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 `\n  --messages file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" `\n  --inference-config file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\"\n```\n\nAWS currently lists Nova 2 Lite as supporting **Converse**, so this is a valid operation; the likely defect in our prior call was the raw model ID rather than the required US inference-profile ID. citeturn758088search0turn758088search3\n\n### Document the finding like this\n\n```text\nTEST:\nbedrock-runtime Converse\nRegion: us-east-2\nModel ID: amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n\nRESULT:\nValidationException — Operation not allowed\n\nCLASSIFICATION:\nINFERENCE ROUTING / MODEL ID\n\nROOT CAUSE CANDIDATE:\nNova 2 Lite in US regions requires US CRIS inference profile:\nus.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n\nNOT INDICATED:\nCredential failure\nNetwork failure\nBilling failure\nRegion connectivity failure\nJSON parsing failure\n```\n\nAnd this is exactly why the earlier Playground behavior was confusing: **“model exists in region” and “raw model ID is directly invokable” are not the same thing anymore.**\n\nRun that `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0` test. If it returns `NOVA_OK`, we've cracked the Amazon-native problem too.", "text_chars": 2738, "code_blocks": 7, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["TEMP\\nova-inference.json", "TEMP\\nova-messages.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2379e7b5-e25b-4ed1-94a0-6351a6597952", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216173.305458, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3b9fb8db-1667-4650-8924-e59a9b6ca24c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790216173.309845, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6d18f1f2-1ccc-451f-a637-5dfbad799624", "role": "user", "role_name": nul